 },
 "parts": [
  "Why does everything cost nine ninety-nine?",
  "When we all know it really means ten.",
  "Your brain reads a price from left to right.",
  "It grabs the first digit before the rest.",
  "So two ninety-nine feels like two-something. Three dollars feels like three.",
  "Researchers call it the left-digit effect.",
  "In three field experiments on real products, a nine ending raised demand every time.",
  "It even works on numbers that are not prices.",
  "In a study of more than twenty-two million used cars sold at auction,",
  "cars just under eighty thousand miles sold for about two hundred ten dollars more than cars just over.",
  "The story you have heard is about cash registers.",
  "Round prices let a cashier skip the till.",
  "It is a nice story. It has not been proven.",
  "A Czech businessman, Tomas Bata, used nine endings widely from nineteen twenty.",
  "People called it Bata's price.",
  "Your defense: round every price up in your head.",
  "Nine ninety-nine is ten."
 ],
